---
import "@skryensya/core/components/changelog.css";
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../../../components/StylingHooks.astro";
import { changelogTree } from "../../../demos/changelog";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core`;

const styles = `@import "@skryensya/core/components/changelog.css";`;
---

<Base
  title="Changelog"
  description="Changelog: a dated history on a rail, where the date is what you came for and the dot says what kind of change it was."
>
  <h1>Changelog</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A dated history, newest first. The date leads each entry because it is what a reader came to
    find, and the dot on the rail is a neutral tick that says <em>when</em>: the <em>what</em> is the
    word beside it. Static: no enhancer and no state.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview tree={changelogTree(t)} label="Changelog" note="four kinds" />

  <h2>When to use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Use Changelog for release notes and for a contract's history: things that happened, each with
      its day.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use <a href="/en/components/process-list">ProcessList</a> for instructions in order. It numbers
      its markers with a CSS <code>counter()</code>, so a newest-first list would number itself
      backwards through time.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use <a href="/en/components/steps">Steps</a> when there is progress: <code>complete</code>,
      <code>current</code>, <code>upcoming</code>. Its connector says how much work is behind the
      reader, which of a shipped change is not a claim anyone can make.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />
  <CodeBlock code={styles} label="CSS" lang="css" />

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The root is <code>&lt;ol class="sk-changelog" reversed&gt;</code>. The <code>reversed</code> is
      fixed rather than an option: newest-first is what a changelog <em>is</em>. Nothing renders the
      numbers, but the accessibility tree reads them, and there they have to count backwards.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The date is two fields.</strong> The <code>date</code> option is the machine-readable
      day (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>) and lands on <code>&lt;time datetime&gt;</code>; the visible one
      is a slot, because a formatted date is copy in a language and Core ships none. Format with
      <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code> and fill the slot.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The kind is two as well.</strong> The <code>kind</code> option marks the entry
      (<code>added</code>, <code>changed</code>, <code>fixed</code>, <code>removed</code>,
      <code>breaking</code>); the slot is the word, and it is required, so the kind is never colour
      alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>target</code> is optional: the option, part or signature the change landed on. An entry
      about the whole contract carries none.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One colour.</strong> Painting each kind its status colour left a rail of green, blue
      and amber down a page whose content is prose, and green won on sheer count: nearly every entry
      in nearly every changelog is an addition. The dot is a neutral tick; the kind is already
      written beside it. <code>breaking</code> is the sole exception, because it is the only kind
      whose cost of being skimmed past is a consumer's build: it takes the dot and the word. To get
      the status palette back, that is one <code>--sk-changelog-marker-color</code> per kind.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Dates, not versions</h2>
  <p>
    This contract has no version field, deliberately. A version number only says something to a
    reader who knows which releases exist; a date reads on its own. Once there are published
    versions, the place for them is the date slot, beside the day rather than instead of it.
  </p>

  <StylingHooks groups={[{ file: "components/changelog.css", probe: "sk-changelog" }]} />
</Base>
